"""Module 2, Lesson 7: SSH & Remote Administration."""

LESSON = {'id': '2-7', 'title': 'SSH & Remote Administration', 'summary': 'Connect to Linux systems safely with SSH. Understand clients and servers, host verification, key-based authentication, ~/.ssh, scp, remote commands, and trustworthy remote administration.', 'duration_minutes': 65, 'xp': 70, 'audio_script': "Welcome to Lesson 2.7: SSH and Remote Administration.\n\nIn DevOps, the Linux system you need is often somewhere else: a cloud virtual machine, a data-center server, a lab host, or a bastion protecting an internal environment. SSH, Secure Shell, is the standard foundation for encrypted remote administration.\n\nThe basic model is client and server. Your workstation runs an SSH client. The remote Linux machine commonly runs OpenSSH sshd. A command such as ssh user@host connects the two, establishes an encrypted channel, authenticates identities, and gives the authorized account a remote shell or remote command execution.\n\nThe first operational habit is context. A remote prompt can look almost identical to your local prompt. Before consequential work, confirm hostname, whoami, id, and pwd. Know which host, account, environment, and directory you are controlling.\n\nSSH also authenticates the server. The first connection to a host may display a host-key fingerprint. Verify that fingerprint through a trusted source or your organization's documented process before accepting it. Accepted host identities are commonly recorded in ~/.ssh/known_hosts.\n\nIf a known host's key unexpectedly changes, do not automatically delete the old record. A legitimate rebuild or key rotation may explain it, but so can reaching the wrong host or an interception attempt. Investigate the change. Evidence Before Action applies to trust warnings.\n\nUser authentication can use passwords, keys, certificates, or other mechanisms. Key-based authentication is common. A key pair has a private key and a public key. The public key can be installed on authorized systems. The private key stays under your control. Never copy it into tickets, chat, documentation, source control, or a remote server merely to log in.\n\nssh-keygen can create a key pair. Ed25519 is a common modern choice where supported. A passphrase can protect the private key, and an SSH agent can hold unlocked key material for a session.\n\nYour ~/.ssh/config file can define aliases and per-host settings such as HostName, User, Port, and IdentityFile. This is more than convenience: explicit configuration can reduce mistakes about which identity and destination you intended.\n\nSSH can also execute a single command without an interactive shell, for example ssh host hostname. This is useful in diagnostics and automation. Remember that remote PATH, environment variables, shell behavior, and working directory may differ from your interactive session.\n\nscp transfers files over SSH. Always confirm which side is the source and which is the destination before copying. You will also encounter sftp and rsync over SSH.\n\nSSH commonly uses TCP port 22, but the port can be changed. A changed port is not a substitute for strong authentication, patching, network controls, and monitoring.\n\nTroubleshoot SSH by layer. A timeout often points toward reachability or filtering. Connection refused often means the destination is reachable but no service is accepting that connection, or the connection is actively rejected. Permission denied during authentication means you reached an SSH server but the requested user authentication or authorization failed.\n\nRemote access increases your reach, not your permission to guess. Orient yourself, gather evidence, make the smallest justified change, verify the outcome, and exit the session cleanly.\n\nFor the lab, inspect SSH safely. You may create a disposable key pair under your Ascend lab directory, but never paste the private key into your Journal. Record only safe metadata such as key type, path, and public-key fingerprint.\n\nSSH is how you often reach the Linux systems you will operate. Trust the destination, protect your credentials, know your context, and diagnose the layer that actually failed.\n\nNext, we will turn repeated shell work into practical Bash automation.\n\nKeep climbing.", 'objectives': ['Explain SSH client/server operation and encrypted remote access.', 'Explain host-key verification and known_hosts.', 'Protect private keys and describe key-based user authentication.', 'Use SSH config, remote commands, and scp concepts safely.', 'Differentiate timeout, refusal, and authentication failures.'], 'content': [{'heading': 'SSH creates a secure remote session', 'body': 'The client connects to sshd, authenticates the server and user, and protects traffic with encryption.'}, {'heading': 'Context is part of command safety', 'body': 'Confirm hostname, user, environment, and working directory before consequential remote work.'}, {'heading': 'Host keys establish server identity', 'body': 'Known host fingerprints help detect unexpected destination changes. Verify changes instead of blindly bypassing warnings.'}, {'heading': 'Private keys are credentials', 'body': 'Public keys may be distributed to authorized destinations; private keys remain protected and out of Git, tickets, logs, and chat.'}, {'heading': 'SSH config reduces ambiguity', 'body': 'Aliases can explicitly bind destinations, users, ports, and identity files.'}, {'heading': 'Errors identify layers', 'body': 'Timeout, refusal, and authentication denial point toward different parts of the connection path.'}, {'heading': 'Remote administration still requires Evidence Before Action', 'body': 'Access to a production shell is not permission to make broad changes. Orient, inspect, hypothesize, change minimally, and verify.'}], 'diagram': {'title': 'SSH creates a secure remote session', 'description': 'Connect to Linux systems safely with SSH. Understand clients and servers, host verification, key-based authentication, ~/.ssh, scp, remote commands, and trustworthy remote administration.', 'nodes': [{'label': 'SSH creates a secure remote session', 'detail': 'The client connects to sshd, authenticates the server and user, and protects traffic with encryption.'}, {'label': 'Context is part of command safety', 'detail': 'Confirm hostname, user, environment, and working directory before consequential remote work.'}, {'label': 'Host keys establish server identity', 'detail': 'Known host fingerprints help detect unexpected destination changes. Verify changes instead of blindly bypassing warnings.'}, {'label': 'Private keys are credentials', 'detail': 'Public keys may be distributed to authorized destinations; private keys remain protected and out of Git, tickets, logs, and chat.'}, {'label': 'SSH config reduces ambiguity', 'detail': 'Aliases can explicitly bind destinations, users, ports, and identity files.'}, {'label': 'Errors identify layers', 'detail': 'Timeout, refusal, and authentication denial point toward different parts of the connection path.'}], 'caption': 'Orient, gather evidence, make the smallest justified change, and verify.'}, 'engineer_perspective': {'title': 'Engineer’s Perspective', 'body': 'Access to a production shell is not permission to make broad changes. Orient, inspect, hypothesize, change minimally, and verify.'}, 'try_it_yourself': {'title': 'Try It Yourself', 'intro': 'Practice in an authorized lab environment and avoid exposing credentials or changing real production services.', 'steps': ['Run ls -la ~/.ssh if it exists; do not display private-key contents.', 'Create a disposable Ed25519 lab key under ~/ascend-linux-lab/ssh if desired.', 'Identify the private and public files without copying their contents into the Journal.', 'View only the public-key fingerprint with ssh-keygen -lf.', 'Write a fictional ~/.ssh/config Host entry using no real credentials.', 'Write a read-only ssh HOST hostname example.'], 'takeaway': 'Explain what each command proves before you use it.'}, 'lab': {'title': 'Lesson 2.7 Lab', 'instructions': ['Run ls -la ~/.ssh if it exists; do not display private-key contents.', 'Create a disposable Ed25519 lab key under ~/ascend-linux-lab/ssh if desired.', 'Identify the private and public files without copying their contents into the Journal.', 'View only the public-key fingerprint with ssh-keygen -lf.', 'Write a fictional ~/.ssh/config Host entry using no real credentials.', 'Write a read-only ssh HOST hostname example.', 'Write an scp example copying a fictional remote diagnostics file to your local machine.', 'For timeout, refused, and publickey-denied errors, identify the first layer you would investigate.', 'List hostname, whoami, id, and pwd as your remote context checks.', 'Write three rules you will follow before changing a remote production system.']}, 'quiz': [{'question': 'What does SSH primarily provide?', 'choices': ['Encrypted authenticated remote access', 'Filesystem formatting', 'Git branching', 'Package compilation'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What is an SSH host key used for?', 'choices': ['Authenticating the remote server', 'Storing shell history', 'Granting root to everyone', 'Compressing files'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Where are accepted host identities commonly recorded?', 'choices': ['~/.ssh/known_hosts', '/tmp/passwords', '~/.bash_history', '/var/log/df'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Which key must remain secret?', 'choices': ['Private key', 'Public key', 'Public fingerprint', 'Key type'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What should you do with an unexpected host-key change?', 'choices': ['Investigate and verify it through a trusted source', 'Delete the warning automatically', 'Disable host checking', 'Commit a new private key'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What can ~/.ssh/config provide?', 'choices': ['Per-host aliases and connection settings', 'Permissions for every Linux file', 'systemd logs', 'CPU scheduling'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What does scp use for secure transfer?', 'choices': ['SSH', 'DNS', 'Git', 'FTP only'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Which error suggests you reached SSH but authentication failed?', 'choices': ['Permission denied (publickey)', 'df: 50%', 'pwd: /home', 'hostname output'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'What should you verify after entering a consequential remote shell?', 'choices': ['Host and user context', 'Terminal font size', 'GitHub stars', 'Battery health'], 'correct': 0}, {'question': 'Why never commit a private SSH key?', 'choices': ['It is a credential that can enable unauthorized authentication', 'Git cannot store text', 'It is always too large', 'Git encrypts it automatically'], 'correct': 0}], 'reflection': 'Why is knowing both the remote host and user part of command safety? Explain how you would verify destination trust and execution context before changing a production service.'}
